---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Disclosure from '../../components/Disclosure.astro';
import EmailCapture from '../../components/EmailCapture.astro';
import { url } from '../../utils/url';

const faqs = [
  { q: 'Does kalkwasser need a stirring reactor?', a: 'No. A covered reservoir can supply clear kalkwasser after undissolved material settles. Keep the intake above the sediment. A reactor is an optional delivery method; follow its instructions and keep solids out of the aquarium.' },
  { q: 'How much kalkwasser powder should I mix?', a: 'Use the instructions for your product and start from measured tank demand. As one product example, Seachem specifies 3 g in 4 L of water, or 6 g in 4 L for its saturated solution. That is not a universal dose for your tank. Dose only the clear liquid and monitor pH and alkalinity.' },
  { q: 'Can I dose kalkwasser with a normal dosing pump?', a: 'Only if the pump maker permits the liquid and the required duty cycle. Use compatible tubing, calibrate the installed line, keep the intake away from sediment, and set a daily volume limit. A low pH reading by itself is not a reason to add more calcium and alkalinity.' },
  { q: 'What should I do if too much kalkwasser is added?', a: 'Stop the addition and prevent further dosing. Check pH, alkalinity and livestock, then seek tank-specific help from an experienced aquarist or the supplement maker before restarting. This guide does not provide an emergency chemical treatment or a guaranteed safe dose.' },
];

const faqLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: f.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
  })),
};

const headline = "Kalkwasser dosing, explained properly";
---
<Base
  headline={headline}
  title="Kalkwasser Dosing: Mixing, Methods & Safety"
  description="Learn how kalkwasser dosing adds calcium and alkalinity. Compare mixing, ATO and metered dosing, pH risks, and when two-part dosing is needed."
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Guides', href: '/guides' }, { name: 'Kalkwasser Dosing', href: '/guides/kalkwasser-dosing' }]}
>
  <section class="container prose" style="padding-top:40px;">
    <p class="eyebrow" style="color:var(--brand-2)">Guide · Dosing</p>
    <h1>{headline}</h1>
    <p class="lede-dark">Kalkwasser adds calcium and alkalinity while raising pH. Use a measured amount of clear liquid, keep undissolved powder out of the tank, and check your tests as demand changes. A stirring reactor is optional.</p>
    <Disclosure />
    <h2>What kalkwasser does</h2>
    <p>Calcium hydroxide mixed with water makes limewater, also called kalkwasser. It can supply calcium and alkalinity, but the amount your tank needs must come from test results. It is a maintenance method, not a reason to chase a pH number with unlimited additions.</p>
    <h2>Mix, settle, use the clear liquid</h2>
    <p>Follow your product's current directions in a separate container. <a href="https://seachem.com/reef-kalkwasser.php">Seachem's Reef Kalkwasser instructions</a>, for example, specify 3 g per 4 L of water, or 6 g per 4 L for a saturated solution. These are product mixing instructions, not a daily tank dose. Do not keep adding powder to make a stronger clear solution.</p>
    <p>Let undissolved material settle. Draw clear liquid from above it and keep the reservoir covered. Do not place the intake in sediment or stir powder into the liquid while it is being drawn into your aquarium. A clear reservoir does not turn back into plain freshwater simply because sediment sits on its bottom.</p>
    <h2>A reactor is optional</h2>
    <p>A covered, settled reservoir is one option. A purpose-built reactor is another. Follow that reactor's mixing and dosing instructions; the aim is controlled delivery of clear solution, not pumping a slurry. Check pump and tubing compatibility before buying hardware.</p>
    <h2>Choose how much to deliver</h2>
    <p><strong>Metered dosing:</strong> set a measured daily total and split its delivery. Re-test pH and alkalinity after changes. Calibrate with the actual tubing and liquid, then check the delivered amount rather than trusting the screen alone.</p>
    <p><strong>Top-off delivery:</strong> evaporation limits how much water can be replaced, but it does not measure calcium or alkalinity use. Changes in room conditions can alter top-off volume without changing coral demand. An ATO failure can also add too much. Keep the amount limited and review tests; top-off is not an automatic safety guarantee.</p>
    <p><strong>pH monitoring:</strong> a compatible controller can be part of a stop system, but a pH cutoff does not replace a daily dose limit or alkalinity tests. Check the complete controller and pump instructions before relying on automation.</p>
    <h2>When two-part may be useful</h2>
    <p>If kalkwasser cannot meet measured demand within your available freshwater volume, compare another supplementation method. Two-part allows separate additions; both methods still need measured demand and calibration. Our <a href={url('/tools/reef-dosing-calculator')}>dosing calculator</a> covers supported two-part products. Do not mix other supplements directly into a kalkwasser reservoir.</p>
    <h2>Before leaving it unattended</h2>
    <p>Check the line for siphoning, keep powder away from its intake, set a volume limit, and confirm what happens after power or connection loss. See the <a href={url('/reviews/best-reef-dosing-pumps')}>dosing pump comparison</a> for controller needs. Stop dosing if results or livestock change unexpectedly, then investigate before restarting.</p>
    <p class="rnote">Source checked October 25, 2026: <a href="https://seachem.com/reef-kalkwasser.php">Seachem's directions</a> require clear liquid, slow addition and pH/alkalinity monitoring. Hardware advice here is a planning checklist, not a claim that any listed pump or controller was tested with kalkwasser.</p>

    <h2>FAQ</h2>
    {faqs.map((f) => (
      <>
        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;">{f.q}</h3>
        <p style="color:var(--muted)">{f.a}</p>
      </>
    ))}
  </section>

  <section class="container">
    <EmailCapture />
  </section>

  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqLd)} />
</Base>

<style>
  .lede-dark { margin-bottom: 8px; }
  .inlinecta { display:inline-block; font-family:var(--font-display); font-weight:700; color:var(--brand-2); }
</style>
